  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1049 PM CDT Wed Apr 22 2026\n\nShowers will wrap up early in the day Saturday, leaving us with \nmostly dry and muggy conditions through Sunday. Temperatures will be \nin the upper 70s to low 80s during this time. By Monday, another \nchance for severe weather will emerge, this time with much more \ndynamic support than Friday's setup. A stout 100 kt upper level \njet developing across the South will drive a negativiely-tilted \ntrough across the Ohio River Valley Monday. At the surface, this \ntranslates to a deepening surface low trekking north over the \nGreat Lakes. A notable change from yesterday's analysis shows that\nthe track of the low pressure center is further north, which \nhelps somewhat reduce the threat for an outbreak of severe \nweather. Still, though, we will need to keep our eyes peeled \nbecause forecast soundings and general pattern recognition do \nfavor an all-hazards severe threat Monday, especially along and \nwest of I-65. \n\nWith all of this being said, the silver lining is that we will get \nat least some relief from the ongoing drought with all of the \nunsettledness. Several inches of rain look likely through next \nweek as additional waves of moisture bring on and off rain\nchances to the area.",
